# Current Editor search and Save browser acceptance

One actual Chrome campaign passed eight finite assertion groups against the installed Editor client/server and31 authenticated current Editor/shared files. Three current public shell files and three shared-host dependencies are separately inventoried. Exact cloned bytes and production before/after hashes remain unchanged. This campaign is distinct from the existing78-test/43-artifact receipt, historical search126 epoch, initial13 search installation, original prepared comparison contexts, and earlier negative/corrected Save evidence; none are rebound or rerun here.

Real saved-file search found3 literal matches in2 files, opened the correct saved position and retained a dirty draft when an older saved search result was clicked. A real Save response was held after the actual host returned200 and direct disk bytes matched its content/revision digest. While held, typing stayed enabled, two duplicate Control+S presses issued no additional Save, switching to B preserved its draft/status after A completed, and A's newer retained draft later saved against the correct new revision.

Two further real committed responses were held. Closing a clean A tab during a same-content Save, reopening it and typing a fresh draft prevented the old response from changing the new document; that draft then saved normally. Navigating away disposed an old Editor view after a different write committed. A fresh Editor view read those committed bytes, accepted a new draft, ignored the old response and saved successfully. Close used a clean tab and needed no native discard dialog. Application navigation intentionally discards old-view drafts; no cross-view draft persistence claim is made. Six browser Save requests cover these interleavings and subsequent writes; six is a request count, not the semantic case count. Two additional Save requests seeded the owned fixture before browser use. The final B disk bytes stayed unchanged.

The private host and relay used ephemeral ports50130 and50133 and synthetic temporary files only. The relay accepted the exact local relay Host/Origin, rewrote them to the actual host's expected values, forwarded request method/path/body/session token unchanged, removed hop-by-hop headers and recomputed content-length. These explicit transformations are retained per request. This is a test-only HTTP policy fixture, not a production proxy or proxy security claim. All22 forwarded requests match22 actual browser host calls; the host saw24 calls including2 seeding requests. Three responses were held only after genuine success and disk authentication, and all were released. The no-favicon404 and absent private Notes-module503 are expected fixture boundaries, retained without classifying them as product faults.

All UI control used documented CUA. Saved AX states, read-only DOM-backed field/focus/selection observations, screenshots, synthetic disk hashes, forwarding records and source copies are retained. No bridge failure or confirmed product defect occurred. The owned tab closed successfully; host and relay closed normally with exit0, confined temporary data was removed, and31 production/clone files plus the host-controller source stayed exact. User4311 was untouched. No ordinary test suite, original Editor GUI/workbench, prepared original comparison context, or benchmark was executed.

Root corrected its earlier shorthand: search12 meant historical original-comparator prepared contexts, not12 current source files. No twelve-source subset was generated. The authoritative current source count is31, with actual filenames, and the eight browser check groups do not imply full Editor parity or performance superiority. The private deployment contains only Editor; navigating to Lab Notes still executes the exact shell's disposal while its missing payload yields the retained expected diagnostic.
